{"kind":"assert","text":"AR-1 condition 2, the cause, read from source at main 447e9b1a (nothing run; the AR-1a gate is running). Two paths re-arm the archive wake with no bound.\n\nPath A, an immediate loop. `ArchiveRows.signal(at)` (archive-row.ts:493-499) stores the wake at the commit time, through `ArchiveDriver.committed()` (archive-driver.ts:120-123) after each committed act. A pass that returns before it writes a wake leaves that past time in place. Those passes are: a pin not yet readable (archive-driver.ts:195, 214, 215), and a policy that is not yet ready, where `preparePolicies` throws `ArchivePolicyPending` (apps/room/archive-packages.ts:111, 144, 199; apps/room/archive.ts:116) and the catch (archive-driver.ts:506) sets only the in-memory `#fallback`. `wake()` (124-132) returns the earlier of the stored wake and `#fallback`, which is the past time. `Wakes.set()` (outbox.ts:81-89) does not clamp it, and `alarm()` re-sets the alarm after every pass (object.ts:974-1003). The alarm therefore fires again at once. The loop runs as fast as a pass completes, and each pass reads installation and policy state from the Directory scope that every room's creator chain reaches (archive.ts:58, 64, 66, 105; archive-packages.ts:88, 102). That fits 2 to 5 wakes a second and about 15 calls a second on one object per Worker.\n\nPath B, a fixed 1 s loop. With no pending Mark, a pass calls `rows.defer(this.#later(0))` before it resolves the target (archive-driver.ts:233-237). `#later(0)` is `dispatchRetrySeconds` = 1 s (bounds.ts:128). A missing or different target returns `retry` at that time (242-245). `defer` keeps no count, so the delay never grows. A room whose target never resolves wakes every second.\n\nProposed fix, one head:\n1. `#later` takes archive bounds: a floor of 60 s and a cap of 3600 s, constants in archive-driver.ts that the note states, replacing the dispatch bounds of 1 s and 300 s. Every archive retry waits at least 60 s. A pass that finds new entries still continues at once, through `#confirmed`.\n2. The wake record gains an optional count of consecutive retries without progress, at most 32. `defer` increments it. Every write that records progress drops it: target, Mark, confirm, clearWake, signal. The delay is 60 s × 2^count, capped at 3600 s.\n3. Every retry return writes its wake through that path, so a stored wake can never stay in the past after a pass: the pin checks, the policy-pending catch, and target-missing. A failed write keeps the in-memory fallback at the floor.\n4. A test over a simulated hour, for an idle room with the archive wired and the target missing or the policy pending, counts alarms and Directory calls. The ceiling: 60, 120, 240, 480, 960 and 1920 s give at most 7 wakes in the first hour, then 1 an hour. Today's code gives about 3600, or one per pass duration.\n5. apps/room/worker.ts returns to `if (!config) return deployed;`.\n\nI start on this now in a new worktree on main, with code only after the AR-1a gate's END. Tell me if you want a different floor or cap.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}